t(42, 155, """here. And we all said with one understanding: First, those who studied religious knowledge are not prophets who do not err, yet people (most of them) follow the way of sanctifying and ascribing infallibility to everyone who wears a turban and grows a beard!

Second, I had written here months ago: most Shia sanctified the Household of the Prophet and held them infallible, and most Sunnis sanctified their sheikhs and held them infallible, except those of the people of the Sunnah whom Allah Almighty guided and protected; so both parties are equal in misguidance! Therefore (((many))) people, not all people, are still like blind followers in religious thought: when a man puts on a turban or a cap and speaks about religion, they say: You have spoken the truth. The cause is the absence of knowledge among people, so they believe by opinion, not by evidence. The man came out and spoke by opinion, and some people think opinion is evidence and text; that too is only from their ignorance, for they do not know the meaning of the word (evidence) and think that what the Prophet ﷺ said is explained by a stunted opinion. They do not know that the knowledge of the end of time is something great and immense that cannot be handled the way the brother in the picture handled it, with this intellectual vulgarity, most regrettably.

And most regrettably, the view becomes clear to us all after a long journey of watching and observing, and it is a view whose concealment is a betrayal of Allah, His Messenger and the believers. The truth that must never be concealed among people is this: specialising in religious knowledge is not proof of the acumen of the person who specialised in religious knowledge. Specialising in religious knowledge is not proof of the sound mind of the person who specialised in religious knowledge, nor of his wisdom and ability to see things correctly. Specialising in religious knowledge is not proof that the person's mind is an analytical, alert, bright mind able to see everything clearly. Specialising in religious knowledge is not proof that the one who specialised is more intelligent than you, or more able than you to understand the religion, if you are mindful of Allah, stand in awe of Him and fear Him, and He opens for you a door of understanding that He did not open for the one who specialised in religious knowledge, even if he spent his whole life studying religious knowledge. Mindfulness of Allah Almighty and fear of Him are a thousand times more important than studying religious knowledge at a university, while how many of those who studied religious knowledge at universities did not apply what they learned from the Book of their Lord and the hadiths of their Prophet ﷺ. Religious knowledge is a crown for the one who learned it, acted on it and was mindful of Allah, so that Allah Almighty opened to him the openings of the knowers, upon knowledge and memorisation; that is a thing of utmost beauty.""")

t(42, 2425, """In continuation of what was discussed yesterday about the mashi'ah of the people of Paradise .. Today we speak (meaning we all gather) about the iradah (will) of the people of the Fire, refuge is sought in Allah Almighty from it for us, for all our offspring and for all our loved ones

What is notable is that what has come regarding the people of the Fire, while they are in the Fire, is that they have (iradah) and not mashi'ah; iradah came regarding them, as in saying "they wanted .. they want" .. Whoever read the two posts I published here in writing yesterday will remember that the people of Paradise""")

t(43, 155, """were given (mashi'ah). We said that iradah is the attribute and mark of worldly life; we in the abode of this world have iradah. We said that man's will is not like the will of Allah Almighty, and we said that the will of the Lord, hallowed be His names, is an effective mashi'ah, whereas man's will in the abode of this world does not guarantee that a thing comes about; and we said that this is one of the traits of living in the deficient abode of this world. And we said (meaning we spoke and discussed together; the "we" here does not mean me the writer, refuge is sought in Allah Almighty), we said that man's will may or may not be fulfilled by Allah Almighty in the abode of this world, while in Paradise it turns into a will fulfilled in everything, which is why it moved from the name iradah to the name mashi'ah. What is apparent, then, to one who reflects on the Book of Allah Almighty is that the people of the Fire move to the Fire and take with them the attribute of will from this world, and they have no mashi'ah, as the verses in the Book of our Great Lord indicate, in that in the Fire they (want). But since the will of the accountable, tested person in the abode of this world may be carried out or prevented, the will of the people of the Fire in the Fire is the opposite of their state when they were in the abode of this world: their will in the Fire is always prevented. Whereas one of them in the abode of this world would want a thing and it would come to him or not, like the believer, the will of one of the people of the Fire while he is in the Fire differs from his will in this world, in that his will in the Fire is rejected always, not sometimes. When they want water they are given scalding water; when they want to get out of it they are returned in it; when they want food they are given zaqqum. So of all their will not a single will is fulfilled.

This is a clarification of the meaning of the will moving with them from the abode of this world to the abode of the Hereafter. It is a will in the sense of wishing for a thing to happen; there is no door of striving in it as with the will of this world, but mere wishing with the fall of all means. If they in the Fire want cold water, there is no room for taking means there so that the will may come to be fulfilled! Whereas in this world they were people of a will subject to means which succeed if Allah wills; but in the Fire Allah withholds His enabling from them. One of them, an unbeliever in this world, would want a thing with a will and strive for it by means, and Allah Almighty would enable him to achieve what he wanted. But in the Fire, were he to want a thousand things in a single moment, not one of them would be fulfilled. This, then, is the reality of the will of the people of the Fire while they are in the Fire.""")

t(43, 1335, """It has come in the report: The Muslims will sleep after the 'Isha prayer and will wake for Fajr, but it will be pitch dark when they wake for the prayer .. So one of them will say to himself: Perhaps I slept only a little, so let me finish my sleep! Then they will wake, each in his house and home, hours later to pray Fajr, and again find the darkness intense and pitch black. So one of them will say to himself: Perhaps I slept only a little, so let me sleep a little and then wake. When one of them wakes he looks at the sky and finds the darkness intense and pitch black again. The report, which I read 25 years ago, most probably in al-Fitan by Nu'aym ibn Hammad, continues: They remain like that for days, I think it is 3 days, until they go out to each other's houses, as the report says, and ask one another in astonishment. When the three days have passed, the sun rises from its west.

In this report there is a very important benefit besides the benefit of showing what precedes the rising of the sun from its west, and it is something children and grandchildren should memorise; perhaps they will be among the people of that hard moment. The second benefit is that this report mentions that the Muslims in the time of the sun's rising from its west (do not pray Fajr in intense pitch darkness as happens today in many mosques)! That is because Islam's second universality will correct the time of Fajr for all Muslims and make them all pray at the time of the true dawn. This report confirms this conclusion! Therefore, although the report is classed as weak, it mentions a matter that a hadith narrator could not make up; for how would one composing a text know that before Islam's second universality many Muslims would pray Fajr in the time of intense pitch darkness?!

Note: Not all the mosques of the Islamic world pray at the wrong time; many of the mosques of the Islamic world pray at the time of the true dawn.""")
